    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54375,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "<p>The macroscopic appearance is similar to that of other gastric cancers.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:42:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54376,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "<p></p>\r\n<p>Gastric adenosquamous carcinoma is composed of an admixture of adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma in variable proportions. The squamous cell component should account for &ge;&nbsp;25% of the tumour {22788926}. The squamous cell component may show all cytological and architectural features known to occur in conventional squamous cell carcinoma of the oesophagus and other sites. Mucin staining is useful for highlighting areas of glandular differentiation; p63/p40 immunohistochemistry may help determine the presence and extent of the squamous cell component.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:42:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54377,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 170,
        "headingText": "<p>Not clinically relevant</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:43:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 371,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54378,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 171,
        "headingText": "<p></p>\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:43:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 374,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54379,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 174,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> True glandular or mucous cell differentiation.</p>\r\n<p><em>Note:</em> The presence of adenoid or microcystic changes within squamous foci of otherwise pure squamous cell carcinoma is not sufficient for the diagnosis.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:43:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 372,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54380,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 172,
        "headingText": "<p>Tumour spread to regional nodes and to liver or peritoneum is common. Lymph node metastases are mostly from the adenocarcinoma component, but the squamous cell component or both components may be seen in the metastases.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:43:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 373,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54381,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 31,
        "headingId": 173,
        "headingText": "<p>Because of the rarity of disease and its generally aggressive nature, no specific prognostic features have been identified. Disease stage at diagnosis is the main prognostic determinant {22788926}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T16:43:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]